A||||8||PaUL||Suspension Geometry  84-87
Z||000000||PaUL||04-27-99||05:59 PM||||Hi,<p>After a long discussion with the Ogre on adjusting front wheel camber, I did some research on the fieros front suspensions shortcomings. I think the main flaw with the orig 84-87 design is excessive kingpin angle. This causes excessive thrust angle in turns. From what I read, when a double a arm suspension is turned 7 degrees as in a typical turn, the wheels tilt to give an effective positive camber, not good for grip.<br>Dialing in more static neg camber will help but at the expense of shortened tire life. The real solution is to reduce the kingpin angle and to do this you need longer upper a arms and a different steering knuckle with the balljoint attaching point further outboard. This is essestially what was done in 88 with the new front suspension. It has reduced kingpin angle and longer upper A arms. I havent seen anyone making replacement knuckles/spindles with diff balljoint attaching pts. but I think these are hard to make.
Z||000001||theogre||04-27-99||07:17 PM||||what i'd like to know..... will the whole 88 front cariage bolt into older frames. I suspect it will, but w/o access to one to even measure, I can't confirm it. At least as I understand it right now.... the whole front susp is just a boltup to the frame. kind of like the old VW Bug frontends were.
Z||000002||SCCA FIERO||05-14-99||12:15 AM||||The 84-87 front end is a bolt in. It was taken from the Chevette. As far as the 88 bolting in,I'm not sure about that. If somebody is looking for better handeling out of their 84-87 Ryane Motorsports makes one. It bolts right in and has differant widths if you want. Ryane claims it to pull 1g in a corner. They also say it has correct anti-dive and no bump-steer. This could be an alternative to the 88 maybe even better. 
