Z||000004||Phil||05-14-99||05:27 PM||||Cost is about $900 www.ryanemotorsports.com
Z||000005||RFMike||05-15-99||04:02 AM||||Yes, I can search thge web, but I was reading this just before leaving for work.  Just read the ryanemotorsports site. My pockets got empty just reading.  The $900 price is not for the complete bolton.  Here is the complete price.<br>       #F053 Slalom Front Suspension system (84-87) standard track width $2250.00<br>       #F039A Optional HAL 12 position externally adjustable aluminum shocks add$222.00<br>       #F059A Optional high performance 1" diam. sway bar $192.00<br>If the 88 bolted on, it would be a whole lot cheaper to find a wreck and scavenge.
Z||000006||John Hansen||05-16-99||09:15 PM||||Hey there,<br>                Just thought I'd share some of what I've experieced after spending MANY hours swapping springs and screwing around in the shop in order to achieve a very fast,compliant fiero; something that wouldn't have a tendancy to break looose in a very high speed corner.First I must commend you on your brakes, sounds like a good setup.The car handled pretty good with no springs at all except for the bump stops,which is what most "performance springs" I tried were like! I'm into adhesion,not sideways,scare your mom stuff.<br>The stock springs totally suck as I'm sure you know,the rears start at too soft of a rate,then get way too stiff.I wound up(after trying many springs) using '87 Mazda  626 front springs in the rear of the Fiero,with Citation struts(lower spring seat,removable strut inserts),spacers(2-3/8") on the top of the springs(way too short of a spring without),and Konis,the strut upper mountings were turned upside down as well,giving a guaranteed full travel.Toe in was 1/8" total at the rear,camber was about 1.5 degrees.I made a set of control arm bushings out of U.H.M.W.(available from quality plastics suppliers),using the sleeves that were bonded into the originals.These were a bitch to take out because they're all molded together with the rubber bushings,had to smooth out the sleeves after they were out with a sander so they didn't tear-up the plastic bushings.This helped get out the squirm that the stock bushings have much of just driving in a straight line,and helped keep the camber where you want it during manuevers.The car actually rode better with this modification and no other changes.Checked the setup for wear after a year of punishment,and they were still without any slop at all.<br>Was going to use a small(1/2")sway bar at the rear as well,never tried it though.Would have made a further improvement in the ride quality too. A 1-1/4"  (or larger) bar would be neat for autocross,but deadly for the high speed stuff.<p>The front end of the car got '71-'82' Cadillac upper ball joints welded to the Fiero lower control arms,racing style.These are longer and raise the spindle about 5/8" or so,lowering the car,without changing the geometry of the lower arm.It raises the upper arm,and reduces bump steer,as a bonus.I was only looking for a replacement for the very poor quality,aftermarket(or dealer) ball joints.The new ones I got were consistantly loose where the stud went into the ball! Total garbage especially on a performance car.The upper ball joints were seventies German Opel from a junkyard;very good metal quality helped these parts to last as long as they did and still be better than the new replacement Fiero ones that were from a Chevette parts bin.Think I used the whole spindle from the opel as well.The Opel steering was a naer bolt in and was next before I quit the project.Springs in the front were Tokico lowering springs from the front of a seventies VWRabbit,again with custom spacers,will have to find my notes to find the exact size.This again was after many combinations of front/rear spring trials conducted on late night,empty freeways.The swaybar(original) was remounted using urethane bushings on the inner mounts,rubber on the outer,and with new longer links to get the bar horizontal again after altering the ride height.Shocks were Bilsteins fronts from a 122S Volvo,mounted upside down for less unsprung wieght.<br>Wow,didn't realize how much stuff I did to that car,it was all worth it however,when one night during the later part of the project,I came across this guy out in the canyons where I'd run, driving a brand new turbo Lotus Esprit.Not only did I stick with him,I almost ran him over! And all with(obviously)more stability and confidence on the bumpy,winding roads than he could ever hope for in his car;I'd be pissed if I'd spent that much and it didn't work any better than that.<br>I'm thinking about getting another Fiero so I can enjoy again what was the best handling car I've ever experienced.I've had many sports cars.<br>Oh,yeah,the tires I used were Yokahama A509s,205/60 in the rear,and some Continentals off of a BMW 325 ,Sport Contacts, 195/65 all on stock rims,total sleeper.  
Z||000007||Pretender||05-17-99||01:46 AM||||i am so glad to have the 88.
Z||000008||Eric||05-17-99||11:39 AM||||The 88 with A509s on the stock rims simplifies things a lot.  Maybe I can't run with a real Lotus, but I still have the comfort in knowing my suspension is about as good as it could be.  Lots of luck to you 84-87 guys.
